{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789832183,"nonce":"WJJktXK+h5gVQyhH86KOboKUXh8=","title":"an Int64 static called on a value drops the value; toInt truncated instead of throwing","message":"`a.toInt()` with `a:Int64` reached the mapping row `static haxe.Int64.toInt` through three probes that, for a static row, drop the receiver and zero-fill the missing parameter: `try_extern_class_method_call`, `try_stdlib_mapped_method_call` and the by-name paths. `a.toInt()` returned 0 for every `a`; `a.neg()` gave 0.\n\nThe `Int64_toInt` MIR wrapper truncated, where Haxe's `toInt` throws \"Overflow\" past Int (TestInt64's `exc(tryOverflow.bind(a))`). Removing the row -- the Haxe body is the specification -- exposed the last-resort scan in `try_stdlib_runtime_dispatch`: every stdlib class is tried by method name and arity, so `toInt` with no arguments became `rayzor.Usize.toInt` (`Usize_toInt`).\n\nFix: a static row reached through a value (not a type) takes the value as its first parameter; the `toInt` row is gone; a symbol that carries its owner's qualified name never enters the all-classes scan or the return-type inference.","files":null}]}